n><span>#<!-- -->model comparison</span></div><h1 class=\"mt-3 text-3xl font-extrabold leading-tight sm:text-4xl\">Cuey compares ChatGPT and Claude and Gemini in a single tab</h1></header><aside aria-label=\"In short\" class=\"mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-muted/40 p-4 sm:p-5\"><div class=\"flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-list-ordered h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M10 12h11\"></path><path d=\"M10 18h11\"></path><path d=\"M10 6h11\"></path><path d=\"M4 10h2\"></path><path d=\"M4 6h1v4\"></path><path d=\"M6 18H4c0-1 2-2 2-3s-1-1.5-2-1\"></path></svg>In short</div><p class=\"mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground/90\" dir=\"ltr\">Cuey sends one prompt to the leading AI models and lines the answers up side by side. It turns the hunch every power user already has — that models are good at different things — into a visible and decision-ready habit.</p></aside><div class=\"prose-blog mt-8\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Type once. Cuey fans the prompt out to the major models and returns the answers in one clean comparison view. No tab juggling and no subscription Tetris — the same question answered several ways with the differences impossible to miss.</p>\n<h2>Why comparing models is now a real workflow</h2>\n<p>The model market has stopped being a one-horse race. Writing quality and reasoning depth and code accuracy all drift apart across providers — and the gap is task-specific rather than general. Experienced users already behave this way by hand. Cuey simply removes the friction of doing it manually.</p>\n<h3>A quiet pricing lesson</h3>\n<p>Seeing answers side by side also makes subscription stacking visible. Most people do not need three premium plans — they need one good default plus a comparison habit for the few tasks where the choice actually matters.</p>\n<p>My own rule after months of daily model use: pick the model per task instead of per brand. The <a href=\"/services\" rel=\"noopener\">idea-to-product pipeline</a> I run works exactly the same way.</p></div><section aria-label=\"My expert take\" class=\"mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-violet-500/40 bg-violet-500/5 p-4 sm:p-6\"><div class=\"flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-bold text-violet-700 dark:text-violet-300\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-graduation-cap h-5 w-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M21.42 10.922a1 1 0 0 0-.019-1.838L12.83 5.18a2 2 0 0 0-1.66 0L2.6 9.08a1 1 0 0 0 0 1.832l8.57 3.908a2 2 0 0 0 1.66 0z\"></path><path d=\"M22 10v6\"></path><path d=\"M6 12.5V16a6 3 0 0 0 12 0v-3.5\"></path></svg>My expert take</div><div class=\"prose-blog mt-2\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Cuey is a small tool in the best sense — it does one job and that job has become strategic. Multi-model comparison is the closest thing to a daily driver for AI power users. If you ever hesitated over which model to trust with a task this is the fastest way to stop guessing. One request for the next version: keep the free tier honest — comparison layers die the moment they turn into ads for one provider.</p></div></section><div class=\"mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-2\"><span data-slot=\"badge\" class=\"inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md border px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium w-fit whitespace-nowrap shrink-0 [&amp;&gt;svg]:size-3 gap-1 [&amp;&gt;svg]:pointer-events-none focus-visible:border-ring focus-visible:ring-ring/50 focus-visible:ring-[3px] aria-invalid:ring-destructive/20 dark:aria-invalid:ring-destructive/40 aria-invalid:border-destructive transition-[color,box-shadow] overflow-hidden [a&amp;]:hover:bg-accen